    "digdeeper": {
      "type": "zeta-accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "a1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Description",
            "content": "<p>ZETA PRO-B is a vegetarian, dairy-free, gluten-free four-strain probiotic delivering 100 billion CFU per capsule. The four strains — B. lactis HN019®, L. acidophilus La-14, L. plantarum Lp-115, and B. longum Bl-05 — are selected for their published research, ability to survive GI transit, and demonstrated adhesion to intestinal epithelial cells. PRO-B uses DRcaps™ gastro-resistant capsules to protect live organisms from stomach acid and ensure targeted release in the small intestine. Each capsule is individually sealed in nitrogen-purged aluminum blister packs to protect against heat, moisture, and oxygen — from the first capsule to the last. One capsule daily.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Ingredients",
            "content": "<ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Bifidobacterium lactis HN019® (HOWARU™)</strong> — The flagship strain. Extensively studied in human RCTs for bowel regularity, GI transit time, and microbiome diversity. Survives GI transit, adheres to epithelial cells, has a 20+ year safety record in published literature.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Lactobacillus acidophilus La-14</strong> — Human-origin strain, classified as type A1 L. acidophilus. Demonstrates excellent adhesion to human epithelial cell lines. Historically present in fermented milks and naturally occurring in human GI flora.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Lactobacillus plantarum Lp-115</strong> — Isolated from plant material; abundant in lactic acid-fermented foods. Demonstrates excellent epithelial adhesion in vitro. Resistant to low pH and survives bile at duodenal concentrations.</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Bifidobacterium longum Bl-05</strong> — Well-established safety profile. Naturally resistant to low pH and bile salts. Well-suited to the intestinal environment with documented performance in the gut.</li>\n<li>Capsule: DRcaps™ vegetarian capsule (hypromellose). No additional fillers, binders, or additives.</li>\n</ul>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Directions & Precautions",
            "content": "<p>Take 1 capsule daily, preferably in the morning. The DRcaps™ capsule handles stomach transit — no need to adjust timing around meals. Consistency matters more than perfect timing. Store at room temperature, away from heat and moisture. Do not refrigerate (unnecessary with nitrogen-purged blister pack storage).</p><p><strong>Precautions:</strong> Probiotics are generally well-tolerated. Some individuals experience mild GI adjustment in the first 1–2 weeks as the microbiome shifts — this is normal. If you are immunocompromised, critically ill, or post-surgical, check with your physician before use. If pregnant or nursing, check with your provider. Not intended for use in infants without medical guidance.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Research",
            "content": "<div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Waller PA et al.</strong> Dose-response effect of Bifidobacterium lactis HN019 on whole gut transit time and functional gastrointestinal symptoms in adults. <em>Scandinavian Journal of Gastroenterology</em> (2011). 100-subject RCT demonstrating statistically significant reduction in GI transit time with daily HN019® supplementation. The clinical anchor for the transit-time claim — and the study that set HN019® apart from other strains.</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Vanderpool C, Yan F, Polk DB.</strong> Mechanisms of probiotic action: Implications for therapeutic applications in inflammatory bowel diseases. <em>Inflammatory Bowel Diseases</em> (2008). Comprehensive review of the seven mechanisms by which probiotics benefit health — metabolic activity, epithelial adhesion, microbial balance, immune function, and more. The scientific framework behind the multi-strain approach.</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Ahmed M et al.</strong> Impact of Bifidobacterium lactis HN019 on intestinal microflora of elderly subjects. <em>Journal of Nutrition Health &amp; Aging</em> (2007). Statistically significant increases in beneficial microflora in subjects over 60 — the demographic where microbiome diversity declines most sharply and supplementation matters most.</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Gopal PK et al.</strong> Effects of Bifidobacterium lactis HN019 and galacto-oligosaccharides on gastrointestinal microflora in human subjects. <em>Nutrition Research</em> (2003). Documents measurable increases in bifidobacteria and lactobacilli following HN019® supplementation — the microbiome population shift that drives downstream benefits.</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Collado MC, Meriluoto J, Salminen S.</strong> Role of commercial probiotic strains against human pathogen adhesion to intestinal mucus. <em>Letters in Applied Microbiology</em> (2007). Establishes competitive adhesion properties of L. plantarum Lp-115 and L. acidophilus La-14 — the mechanism by which beneficial strains displace less desirable organisms from intestinal tissue.</div>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
      },
